1) @SERVICE  

@Service marks a class so Spring treats it as a service.  

In most apps, the service part holds the work rules and decisions.  
I feel the controller should stay light and only call other parts.  
The controller sends input to the service. The service then does the real steps.  

Flow I noted:  
Controller  
↓  
Service  
↓  
Repository  

2) SERVICE LAYER  

A service acts like a go between.  
It sits between the controller and the repository.  

Example flow:  
Controller receives a request  
↓  
Service reads and processes Employee data  
↓  
Repository fetches or updates data in the database  

So I keep it simple:  
Service means business logic  

3) @REPOSITORY  

@Repository marks a class for the repository role.  

This layer is for database tasks.  
It handles actions like:  
get data  
save data  
update data  
delete data  

Flow I wrote:  
Controller  
↓  
Service  
↓  
Repository  
↓  
Database  

4) EMPLOYEE DATA  

We used Employee data to learn Streams.  

An Employee can hold:  
id  
name  
department  
salary  

Sample objects:  
Employee(1, "Manohar", "IT", 50000)  
Employee(2, "Rahul", "HR", 40000)  
Employee(3, "Suresh", "IT", 70000)  

We can store them in a list:  
List  

5) LIST  

List is a list of Employee objects.  

Example:  
List employees = new ArrayList<>();  

employees.add(employee1);  
employees.add(employee2);  
employees.add(employee3);  

After that, we can work on the list using Java Streams.  

6) JAVA STREAMS  

A Stream helps deal with collection data.  

Instead of using many loops, Stream API lets us apply steps to the data.  

Typical methods:  
stream()  
filter()  
map()  
collect()
